(*!
<summary>Defines a permission rule for an action, its requester and the object evaluating the request.</summary>
<description>
Each implementation defines how it recognizes actions: by name, a specialized descriptor interface,
or descriptor instance identity. Descriptor names are not required to be globally unique.
Callers must supply descriptors compatible with the selected policy's recognition rules.
</description>
*)

INTERFACE IActionPermissionPolicy EXTENDS IObject]]></Declaration>
    <Method Name="CheckActionPermission" Id="{e90b0c96-c28a-4c1c-ac59-5d493afa0b94}">
      <Declaration><![CDATA[(*!
<summary>Returns whether the policy permits the requested action.</summary>
<description>
The policy only evaluates permission and writes rejectReason; it does not execute the action or change equipment state.
An ordinary rejection returns FALSE. An exception indicates a failure to evaluate permission and propagates to the caller;
it must not be converted into an ordinary rejection. No decision or rejection reason is guaranteed on an exception.
The decision is completed within this call; no continuation in later PLC cycles is required.
Input references and the reason buffer are borrowed only for the duration of the call.
Implementations must not retain them for use after returning. No ownership is transferred.
actionDescriptor, requestingObject and evaluatingObject must be non-null references to live objects.
Defensive null handling in an implementation does not make null arguments valid under this contract.
On FALSE, writes the current rejection reason to rejectReason.
On TRUE, rejectReason is unspecified and need not be cleared; callers must ignore it.
</description>
*)

//!<param name="rejectReason">Receives the current reason on rejection; meaningful only when the result is FALSE.</param>
METHOD CheckActionPermission : BOOL
VAR_INPUT
	actionDescriptor : INamed;
	requestingObject : IObject;
	evaluatingObject : IObject;
END_VAR
VAR_IN_OUT
	rejectReason : WSTRING(255);
END_VAR]]></Declaration>
    </Method>
  </Itf>
</TcPlcObject>
